WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 299: King John: Runnymede, Magna Carta, and the Lost Crown Jewels in the Wash

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:14.080
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the sovereign universally branded the worst king in British history.

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:21.360
[Rebecca] The suspicious, cruel monarch, whose extortionate misrule inadvertently produced the cornerstone of modern human rights.

5
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.280
[Rebecca] Here is George with the dramatic life of King John.

6
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:33.920
[George] Born on Christmas Eve 1166 at Beaumont Palace in Oxford, John was the youngest of Henry I.

7
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:46.800
[George] Because all the major Angevin duchies and earldoms had already been apportioned to his older brothers, his father playfully gave him the derisive nickname that haunted him for life.

8
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.440
[George] Jean Sands Terre, John Lackland, pampered by his father.

9
00:00:51.440 --> 00:01:00.080
[George] John was given the Lordship of Ireland at age 18, where his disastrous campaign in 1185 was marked by juvenile arrogance.

10
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:10.400
[George] John insulted Irish chieftains by pulling their long traditional beards, squandered his army's funds on banquets, and was recalled in disgrace within six months.

11
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:25.520
[George] When King Henry died brokenhearted upon learning John had betrayed him to Richard, and when John later attempted to seize the throne while Richard was imprisoned in Germany, John earned a reputation as a fickle, untrustworthy schemer whose word was worth nothing.

12
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.080
[George] Growing up as the pampered baby of Henry I.

13
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.160
[George] Would later destroy his kingship.

14
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.640
[George] Juvenile cruelty, arrogance, and financial irresponsibility.

15
00:01:51.640 --> 00:02:06.760
[George] When John's older brother King Richard went on crusade in 1190, John spent four years actively plotting with King Philip Augustus of France to seize the English crown, circulating false rumors of Richard's death and bribing Emperor Henry V.

16
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:16.280
[George] I to keep his brother locked in an Austrian dungeon forever, earning the eternal disgust of the chivalric baronage who viewed John as an unprincipled traitor.

17
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:30.360
[George] When Richard died childless in 1199, the Angevin succession was contested between 32-year-old John and his twelve-year-old nephew, Prince Arthur of Brittany, the son of John's deceased elder brother Geoffrey.

18
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.840
[George] Supported by French King Philip Augustus, Arthur was captured by John at Myerbo in 1202.

19
00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:43.160
[George] What followed was an atrocity that permanently destroyed John's standing among the chivalric nobility of Europe.

20
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.360
[George] Arthur was imprisoned in the dungeon of Rouen Castle.

21
00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:59.000
[George] Where contemporary chroniclers recorded that in April 1203, in a drunken fury, King John murdered the boy with his own hands, weighted the corpse with a heavy stone, and cast him into the river Seine.

22
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.320
[George] Disgusted by Arthur's murder, the Norman barons abandoned John.

23
00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:15.400
[George] In 1204, Philip Augustus conquered Shaitao Gaylard and marched into Rouen, wiping out 140 years of Norman ducal rule and reducing John to his English island kingdom.

24
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:23.800
[George] The sinister disappearance of sixteen-year-old Prince Arthur of Brittany in April 1203 was the fatal turning point of John's reign.

25
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:39.320
[George] Chroniclers across France recorded that John, blind, drunk and maddened by suspicion, personally slit his nephew's throat in the dungeon of Rouen Castle, weighted the boy's corpse with a heavy millstone, and cast him into the dark waters of the Seine.

26
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:50.480
[George] When fishermen pulled the mutilated body from the river and the truth leaked out, the barons of Normandy, Anjou and Maine renounced their allegiance to a king they viewed as a child murderer.

27
00:03:50.480 --> 00:04:05.840
[George] Philip Augustus declared John's continental fief's forfeit, capturing the mighty fortress of Shaitao-Gaylard after a five-month siege in 1204, reducing the Angevin Empire to a memory and earning John the humiliating nickname John Softsword.

28
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:16.400
[George] Retreating to England, John entered a titanic clash with the most powerful pope of the medieval era, Innocent III, over the election of the Archbishop of Canterbury.

29
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:27.840
[George] When Innocent consecrated the brilliant English theologian Stephen Langton, John flew into a rage, refusing to let Langton set foot in England and seizing all archbishopric revenues.

30
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:34.560
[George] In March 1208, Pope Innocent retaliated by laying England under a terrifying papal interdict.

31
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:45.680
[George] Church bells were silenced across the kingdom, church doors were locked, churchyards were closed, and the dead were buried like dogs in roadside ditches without Christian funeral rites.

32
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.840
[George] For five years, infants went unbaptized and marriages were celebrated only in church porches.

33
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.160
[George] In 1209, Innocent excommunicated John personally.

34
00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:02.480
[George] Unbothered, John pocketed over 100,000 silver pounds from confiscated monastic lands.

35
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.040
[George] Until the threat of a French invasion in 1213 forced him to grovel.

36
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:14.800
[George] Kneeling before the papal legate, John surrendered England and Ireland to the Pope, receiving them back as a papal feudal vassal.

37
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:23.680
[George] The five-year papal interdict that paralyzed England from 1208 to 1213 transformed the kingdom into a desolate spiritual graveyard.

38
00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:35.560
[George] Church bells ceased ringing, church doors were boarded up with planks, and terrified peasants watched their deceased parents buried in roadside ditches without prayers or consecrated ground.

39
00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:40.840
[George] John used the spiritual crisis to launch an unprecedented financial extortion racket.

40
00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:57.480
[George] He seized the lands, livestock, and grain stores of every bishop and abbot who obeyed the Pope, extorting over 100,000 silver pounds, and even kidnapped the concubines and housekeepers of parish priests, demanding heavy cash ransoms for their release.

41
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:08.600
[George] Only when Pope Innocent Ii threatened to depose John and invite the French king to lead a holy crusade against England did John grovel, submitting his kingdom to papal vassalage.

42
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:16.600
[George] Determined to reconquer Normandy, John squeezed every drop of wealth out of his English subjects through unprecedented feudal extortion.

43
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:30.200
[George] He levied eleven skewtages, taxes paid by barons in lieu of military service, at crushing rates, demanded astronomical inheritance reliefs, and used royal forest laws to impose ruinous fines.

44
00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.200
[George] His cruelty was sadistic.

45
00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:45.880
[George] When nobleman William de Brayas fell out of favour, John starved Brayaz's wife Maude and eldest son to death in the dungeons of Korf Castle, their corpses found with Maude having gnawed on her son's cheeks in desperate delirium.

46
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:54.760
[George] In July 1214, John's grand coalition to reconquer France was decisively crushed by Philip Augustus at the Battle of Bouvins.

47
00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:08.120
[George] Returning to England, humiliated and bankrupt, John was confronted by furious barons who renounced their homage, march on London, and captured the city in May 1215, demanding a formal charter of liberties.

48
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:13.480
[George] John's domestic extortion reached sadistic extremes in the years leading up to Runnymede.

49
00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:23.680
[George] He demanded ruinous inheritance taxes from young widows, auctioned off aristocratic heiresses to the highest bidders, and levied eleven crushing scotages in sixteen years.

50
00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:38.640
[George] His horrific murder of Maude de Brayas and her eldest son in the dungeons of Corf Castle, starving them to death in an unlit cell, terrified the English nobility, convincing them that no baron's family was safe from John's psychopathic whims.

51
00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:50.560
[George] When his grand European coalition to destroy Philip Augustus was smashed at the Battle of Bouvins in July 1214, John returned to England bankrupt, discredited, and defenseless.

52
00:07:50.560 --> 00:08:01.760
[George] The rebel barons, styling themselves the Army of God and Holy Church, marched into London in May 1215, capturing the capital and forcing the tyrant to negotiate.

53
00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:15.600
[George] On Monday, June 15, 1215, on the marshy, watermeadows of Runnymede on the south bank of the Thames near Windsor, King John met the rebel barons led by Robert Fitzwalter and Archbishop Stephen Langton.

54
00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:31.520
[George] Trapped without military leverage, smiling falsely while seething with inward rage, John authorised his clerks to affix the great seal of England to the articles of the barons, which was formally drafted into the 63 clauses of Magna Carta, the Great Charter.

55
00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:41.040
[George] While primarily intended to protect baronial privileges, clauses 39 and 40 contained the immortal words that changed the course of human history.

56
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:48.080
[George] No free man shall be seized or imprisoned, or stripped of his rights or possessions, or outlawed or exiled.

57
00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.320
[George] Except by the lawful judgment of his equals or by the law of the land.

58
00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.520
[George] To no one will we sell, to no one will we deny or delay right or justice.

59
00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:02.200
[George] The divine right of absolute monarchy was forever subordinated to the law.

60
00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:08.200
[George] The historic assembly on the wet meadow of Runnymede was a masterclass in tense political coercion.

61
00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:16.120
[George] King John, camped with a handful of retainers, faced over forty armed rebel barons surrounded by hundreds of mailed knights.

62
00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:24.760
[George] The 63 clauses of Magna Carta, drafted by Archbishop Stephen Langton, established revolutionary constitutional principles.

63
00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:33.880
[George] Clause 12 established that no scootage or aid could be levied without common counsel of the realm, creating the foundation of parliamentary taxation.

64
00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:42.760
[George] Clauses 39 and 40 established the universal right to due process, habeas corpus, and trial by a jury of one's peers.

65
00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:57.320
[George] Clause 61, the notorious security clause, established an elected council of twenty-five barons with the legal right to seize the king's castles and lands if he violated the charter, officially subordinating the sovereign to the rule of law.

66
00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.520
[George] Magna Carta brought peace for barely two months.

67
00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:12.360
[George] As soon as the barons dispersed, John sent secret appeals to his overlord, Pope Innocent III, who issued a papal bull annulling Magna Carta as shameful and illegal under duress.

68
00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:14.920
[George] Civil war erupted across England.

69
00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:23.640
[George] Desperate rebel barons invited Prince Louis of France to invade and claim the English crown, with Louis landing in Kent and occupying London.

70
00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:31.960
[George] In October 1216, as John marched his army north through Lincolnshire, disaster struck at the tidal estuary known as the Wash.

71
00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:40.760
[George] While crossing the treacherous sandbanks between Cross Keys and Sutton Bridge, John's baggage train was overtaken by the roaring incoming tide.

72
00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:45.000
[George] Royal wagons, packhorses, gold chalices, holy relics.

73
00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:52.560
[George] And the ancient Anglo Saxon crown jewels of England were swept away and swallowed forever by the freezing mud and water.

74
00:10:52.560 --> 00:11:00.320
[George] The catastrophic disaster at the wash in October 1216 was the physical manifestation of King John's unraveling reign.

75
00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:16.800
[George] Marching across the treacherous tidal estuary of the wellstream between Cross Keys and Sutton Bridge, John and his light vanguard crossed safely, but his heavy baggage train, wagons laden with gold plate, silver bullion, royal wardrobes, holy relics.

76
00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:22.880
[George] And the ancient Saxon crowns of England was caught in the quicksands by the roaring incoming boar tide.

77
00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:31.680
[George] In minutes, horses screamed, wagons overturned, and the entire royal treasure of the Plantagenet dynasty was swallowed into the freezing mud.

78
00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:39.520
[George] The loss shattered John's fragile mental composure, leaving him wandering like an abandoned ghost through the Lincolnshire Fens.

79
00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:55.520
[George] Devastated by the catastrophic loss of his baggage train and suffering from a violent, agonizing bout of dysentery, worsened by gorging on fresh peaches and raw, unfermented cider at Swineshead Abbey, King John was carried in a horse litter to Newark Castle.

80
00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:06.080
[George] Dying on the stormy midnight of October 18, 1216, at age 49, John asked to be buried in Worcester Cathedral beside the Saxon Saint Wolfston.

81
00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:08.480
[George] His death saved the English monarchy.

82
00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:19.600
[George] His nine year old son was crowned King Henry III under the regency of the legendary knight William Marshall, who immediately reissued Magna Carta to unite the realm and expel the French.

83
00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:26.080
[George] King John went down in history as a cowardly tyrant, a faithless brother, and a murderous uncle.

84
00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:36.600
[George] Yet through his very villainy, he provoked the birth of constitutional liberty, leaving behind the charter that guarantees that no king, president, or sovereign is ever above the law.

85
00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:51.000
[George] Sick with a fatal intestinal inflammation brought on by dysentery and compounded by gorging on fresh peaches and unfermented green cider at Swineshead Abbey, John was carried in agony to Newark Castle amid screaming autumn gales.

86
00:12:51.000 --> 00:13:02.120
[George] Passing away on October 18, 1216, at age 49, John was buried in Worcester Cathedral, dressed in royal vestments beneath an uncarved stone slab.

87
00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:13.320
[George] Historians agree that King John was a cruel, suspicious, and treacherous tyrant whose military failures and domestic extortions brought the English monarchy to the brink of annihilation.

88
00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:25.720
[George] Yet through his very villainy, he forced the birth of Magna Carta, the immortal charter that remains the foundational cornerstone of constitutional liberty and human rights across the globe.

89
00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:29.240
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

90
00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:37.720
[Rebecca] King John's extortions and the immortal clauses of Magna Carta at Runnymede permanently redefine the relationship between the crown and the law.

91
00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:46.600
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with his son King Henry III, The Barons' War, Simon de Montfort, and the birth of the English Parliament.

92
00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:49.080
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

